Rust-Readability contains native translations and adapted data from:

- Go-ReadabilityV2, the core-only fork of Readeck Go Readability v2.1.2,
  upstream commit b18540d99ebf105cd67122585a0a41ec299b70bc, under the MIT
  terms retained in LICENSE. The fork's source receipt is in testdata/go-source.json.
- Go and golang.org/x/net, including HTML entities, rendering, doctypes, atom
  names, URL behavior, sorting, time layouts and Windows timezone abbreviations,
  under the BSD-3-Clause terms in licenses/LICENSE-go.txt.
- github.com/itlightning/dateparse v0.2.1, under the MIT terms in
  licenses/LICENSE-dateparse.txt.
- html5ever 0.39.0, commit ce64836c685025a5fef0860fa2e9c80b2683e8d0,
  for the private HTML tokenizer and supporting macros. Original copyright
  notices are retained in those files. The MIT and alternative Apache-2.0
  texts are retained in licenses/LICENSE-html5ever-MIT.txt and
  licenses/LICENSE-html5ever-APACHE.txt; this package uses the MIT option.
- Rust-DomDistiller 1.0.0 and its Go-Shiori DOM, chardet and ICU-derived reader
  sources and tables. Their original notices are retained in licenses/ and
  their archive/file digests are recorded in testdata/reader-provenance.json.
- The optional benchmark tooling adapts scoring and harness patterns from
  content-extractor-benchmark, commit 466fdbee8a504441eb78ed11d71c1da220681cab,
  and Rust-DomDistiller's benchmark. The original Apache-2.0 benchmark notice
  is retained in licenses/LICENSE-benchmark.txt. It does not relicense the
  source website pages, which are not included here.

The bundled zoneinfo.zip is the exact timezone-data archive shipped with
Go 1.27.1. It contains compiled IANA Time Zone Database data. The Go Windows
abbreviation table is generated from its CLDR-derived toolchain source.

Rust registry dependencies retain their own licenses. Original website pages
used by optional differential tests are not included or relicensed in this
package. UPSTREAM.md records the reference graph, adaptations and evidence.